
docker container:
https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/cloud-computing/what-is-dockerfile/

how to create your own docker container and push it on the dock hub in
springboot like application:

dockerfile------>(build)---->dockerImage----->(run)--->dockerContainer

1. Create the Dockerfile->>>The Dockerfile uses DSL (Domain Specific Language)
 and contains instructions for generating a Docker image.
In the root of your Spring Boot project (where pom.xml or build.gradle is),
create a file named Dockerfile:
# Use a lightweight OpenJDK image
FROM openjdk:17-jdk-slim

# Set the working directory
WORKDIR /app

# Add the jar file (built app) to the image
ARG JAR_FILE=target/*.jar
COPY ${JAR_FILE} app.jar

# Expose the port your app runs on
EXPOSE 8080

# Command to run the jar file
ENTRYPOINT ["java", "-jar", "app.jar"]

Build the Spring Boot App


2. Build the Spring Boot App
Use Maven or Gradle to build your .jar file:
Maven:./mvnw clean package

The output will be in the target/ or build/libs/ directory.

3. Build the Docker Image--->An artifact with several layers and a lightweight,
 compact stand-alone executable package that contains all of the components
required to run a piece of software,including the code, a runtime, libraries,
environment variables, and configuration files is called a Docker image.
docker build -t your-dockerhub-username/your-app-name .
docker build -t jenkins:1 .->Build the image by using the below command with the
help of Dockerfile and give the necessary tags. and the dot(.)
represents the current directory which is a path for Dockerfile.

Replace your-dockerhub-username and your-app-name accordingly.

Run the Docker Image (Optional Test)--->A container is a runtime
instance of an image. Containers make development and deployment more efficient
since they contain all the dependencies and parameters needed for the application
it runs completely isolated from the host environment.
Run the container with the help image ID or tag of the image by using the below command.
docker run -d -p 8080:8080 <Imagetag/ID>
docker run -p 8080:8080 your-dockerhub-username/your-app-name


Visit http://localhost:8080 to verify it works.

pushing into docker hub:
Login to Docker Hub
docker login

docker push your-dockerhub-username/your-app-name
docker push johndoe/springboot-hello


Now anyone can pull and run your Spring Boot app with:
docker pull johndoe/springboot-hello
docker run -p 8080:8080 johndoe/springboot-hello


Docker Compose:
Docker Compose is useful if you want to run your app along with other
services like a database (e.g. PostgresSQL, MySQL) or to simplify
running multi-container environments.



 Full Example: Spring Boot App + Docker Compose
Let’s continue using our previous "Hello, Docker World!" Spring Boot app.

Step 1: Build the Spring Boot JAR
./mvnw clean package

Step 2: Dockerfile
Ensure you have this in your project root:
# Use a lightweight JDK base image
FROM openjdk:17-jdk-slim

# Set working directory
WORKDIR /app

# Copy the JAR
COPY target/demo-0.0.1-SNAPSHOT.jar app.jar

# Expose Spring Boot default port
EXPOSE 8080

# Command to run the JAR
ENTRYPOINT ["java", "-jar", "app.jar"]

Step 3: docker-compose.yml
Create a file named docker-compose.yml in the root:

version: '3.8'

services:
  springboot-app:
    build: .
    container_name: springboot-container
    ports:
      - "8080:8080"



Bonus: Compose with Database (Example)
Want to add a PostgreSQL database?

version: '3.8'

services:
  db:
    image: postgres:14
    environment:
      POSTGRES_DB: demo
      POSTGRES_USER: user
      POSTGRES_PASSWORD: pass
    ports:
      - "5432:5432"

  springboot-app:
    build: .
    container_name: springboot-container
    ports:
      - "8080:8080"
    depends_on:
      - db
    environment:
      SPRING_DATASOURCE_URL: jdbc:postgresql://db:5432/demo
      SPRING_DATASOURCE_USERNAME: user
      SPRING_DATASOURCE_PASSWORD: pass

In your application.properties:
spring.datasource.url=${SPRING_DATASOURCE_URL}
spring.datasource.username=${SPRING_DATASOURCE_USERNAME}
spring.datasource.password=${SPRING_DATASOURCE_PASSWORD}

Then run:
docker-compose up --build

how to combine two docker container using docker compose
To combine two Docker containers using Docker Compose, you define
both services in a single docker-compose.yml file.
This is ideal when you want a Spring Boot app to work alongside another service,
like PostgresSQL, MySQL, Redis, or MongoDB.

Let’s say:
One container is your Spring Boot app
Another is a PostgreSQL database

1. docker-compose.yml

version: '3.8'

services:
  postgres:
    image: postgres:15
    container_name: postgres-db
    environment:
      POSTGRES_DB: mydb
      POSTGRES_USER: user
      POSTGRES_PASSWORD: pass
    ports:
      - "5432:5432"
    volumes:
      - postgres_data:/var/lib/postgresql/data

  springboot-app:
    build: .
    container_name: springboot-app
    ports:
      - "8080:8080"
    depends_on:
      - postgres
    environment:
      SPRING_DATASOURCE_URL: jdbc:postgresql://postgres:5432/mydb
      SPRING_DATASOURCE_USERNAME: user
      SPRING_DATASOURCE_PASSWORD: pass

volumes:
  postgres_data:
